Miadi DeepDiver — user setup
===========================
Installed CLI: /usr/bin/deepdiver, private Python venv, pinned fork and wheels.
Provenance: /usr/share/miadi-deepdiver/SOURCE.json (not PyPI deepdiver 0.1.1).

As the desktop user, not root:
  miadi-deepdiver setup --claude
  miadi-deepdiver doctor --offline --claude
  deepdiver chrome launch
  # SIGN-IN NEEDED: a human signs in to their own Google account in this window.
  deepdiver chrome status
  miadi-deepdiver doctor --claude

Claude Code is installed/authenticated separately. A Claude Code too old to
list plugins is brought forward with its own claude install stable, and the old
version stays on disk. setup --claude installs the
existing miadi-deepdiver@miadi-orchestration-kit marketplace plugin and the
fork's embedded skills with deepdiver skills install --agent claude. Existing
config, disabled plugin settings and differing personal skills are preserved.
Both commands then report what /deepdive reaches beyond this CLI, as OK factory
or FACTORY NEEDS with its remedy: node, ffmpeg, the chronicle checkout, the API
writer token, the episode door, the miadi-review client and Jev. These never
block plain notebooks. Restart Claude to load plugin hooks. The kit's screenwalk-notebook skill and
plan-before-render gate stay canonical; read the fork manual with
  deepdiver skills show notebooklm-automation

setup alone does not launch Chrome or sign in. Config is mode 600 and only
created if absent; ~/.chrome-deepdiver-apt is the persistent APT profile.
Existing ~/.chrome-deepdiver or personal Chrome profiles are not opened or
migrated automatically: Google Chrome and Chrome for Testing can use different
keyring names, so compatibility with old sign-ins is not claimed. No maintainer script
writes a user's home. Removal/purge never remove profiles, credentials, skills
or config. Doctor does not print config values, page URLs or credentials.

Supported: amd64 Ubuntu 22.04 (Python 3.10), Debian 12 (3.11), Ubuntu 24.04
(3.12). Python 3.13+, arm64 and Termux are not packaged by this release.
Ubuntu's chromium-browser is a snap transition, so Playwright's exact captured
Chrome for Testing + headless shell is private under /usr/lib/miadi-deepdiver.
It does not replace your normal browser. The browser-installer package fetches
hash-pinned browser archives directly from upstream at first configuration,
then caches them for offline reinstall. Python wheels are always installed
offline. Browser binaries and raw fork debug captures are not redistributed
by the Debian artifacts. Upstream browser terms/notices apply; see copyright.
First configuration downloads about 328 MB and retains about 1 GB including
browser files and cached archives. If upstream is unavailable, the package
fails closed and later APT operations can also report a half-configured state.
Restore network/CA trust/cache, then run sudo dpkg --configure -a. To abandon
this install: sudo dpkg --remove deepdiver miadi-deepdiver miadi-deepdiver-chromium.
User config/profiles remain. A proxy set only in apt.conf does not configure
this downloader: use the standard https_proxy/http_proxy environment, preserved
explicitly if needed with sudo --preserve-env=https_proxy,http_proxy,no_proxy
dpkg --configure -a. Never print proxy credentials in diagnostic logs.

CDP binds 127.0.0.1:9222, with no remote-debugging wildcard. Local users can
reach CDP: do not expose it, forward it to other hosts or reuse a sensitive
personal browsing profile. No --no-sandbox is installed. Chromium's standard
root-owned setuid sandbox is retained for hosts restricting user namespaces.
If a hardened kernel/AppArmor/container disallows both sandbox mechanisms,
report BLOCKED rather than disabling sandboxing. DISPLAY/XAUTHORITY must name
a reachable X desktop. A Wayland-only session requires XWayland; SSH-only
machines can run offline headless tests, but a person still needs a desktop
for sign-in. A headless test is not proof of Google sign-in or NotebookLM E2E.

  miadi-deepdiver doctor --offline --browser-smoke

Browser versions are deliberately pinned, not silently updated. Maintainers
must refresh source/dependency/browser locks, review security updates, bump
Debian versions and rebuild/retest/release. Upgrades rebuild only the private
venv from hash-verified packaged wheels, with --no-index and no system pip.

Old versions and manual installations
------------------------------------
APT replaces old package files and rebuilds/removes the old private runtime.
setup/doctor also detect other deepdiver launchers on PATH, refusing false
readiness when an old manual copy may shadow /usr/bin/deepdiver. As the user:
  miadi-deepdiver setup --remove-legacy
  hash -r
  type -a deepdiver
The explicit cleanup uninstalls only a verified DeepDiver pip distribution in
a caller-owned home venv. It does not remove that venv, its other dependencies,
config, output or profiles. Recorded legacy files are privately backed up to
~/.local/state/miadi-deepdiver/legacy before uninstall, including edited package
templates. Unknown wrappers, shared/system Python and other
install managers are preserved and BLOCKED with a diagnostic: use their own
uninstaller. No root maintainer script scans or edits home directories.
